################ ZT-008-e_qcstatem_mandatory_etsi_statems
== the two certificates
   subject: FNMT-RCM 'AC Componentes Informaticos' -- the CA and the
            profile of Mozilla bug 1625421, from 
   control: pdsAllHttps.pem -- zlint's OWN test fixture.
   Neither carries QcCompliance. Both carry other ETSI ESI statements.
   subject.pem                                issuer C=ES, O=FNMT-RCM, OU=AC Componentes Informáticos
   ZT-008-control-qualified-policy.pem        issuer CN=Lint CA, O=Lint, C=DE

== the certificate policy each asserts, which is the whole question
   subject.pem                                0.4.0.2042.1.7 
   ZT-008-control-qualified-policy.pem        0.4.0.194112.1.1 
   0.4.0.2042.1.7  is OVCP, from EN 319 411-1  -- NOT qualified
   0.4.0.194112.1.1 is QCP-n, from EN 319 411-2 --     qualified

== what the lint says about each
   subject.pem                                {"e_qcstatem_mandatory_etsi_statems":{"result":"error","details":"missing mandatory ETSI QC statement"}}
   ZT-008-control-qualified-policy.pem        {"e_qcstatem_mandatory_etsi_statems":{"result":"error","details":"missing mandatory ETSI QC statement"}}

   observed  error on both, with identical details.
   correct   pass on the OVCP certificate, which QCS-5-01 does not
             govern; error on the QCP-n one, which it does.

   The two differ only in the arc of the policy they assert, and the
   lint's verdict does not move -- which is the mechanism: CheckApplies
   never reads certificatePolicies at all.

== fix
   Gate CheckApplies on the certificate claiming EU qualified status:
   an EN 319 411-2 policy identifier (arc 0.4.0.194112.1.), which is
   the only claim available to a certificate that lacks QcCompliance.

