################ ZT-059-e_ext_cannot_be_empty_sequence
== 1. zlint's own fixture, unmodified
   frshCRLNotCritical.pem carries SubjectInformationAccess and FreshestCRL
   with byte-identical malformed contents (extnValue 0201 in both), so two
   of the ten target extensions fail. Only one is ever reported.
   outcomes:

   observed  the extension NAMED changes between runs.
   correct   a stable report. Both extensions are defective and neither is
             more the answer than the other -- report both, or iterate in
             a defined order and say so.

   The corpus has exactly two such certificates and both are zlint's own
   fixtures: frshCRLCritical.pem and frshCRLNotCritical.pem.

== 2. a constructed certificate, where the STATUS moves too
   Recipe: a self-signed certificate carrying
     SubjectInformationAccess = 3000   (a well-formed EMPTY SEQUENCE
                                        -> parses, len 0 -> lint.Error)
     FreshestCRL              = 020101 (an INTEGER where a SEQUENCE
                                        belongs -> lint.Fatal)
   Whichever the map reaches first decides the status of the whole lint.
   outcomes:

   observed  error and fatal, from one binary on one certificate.
   correct   one status, deterministically.

   Two consumers reading this lint disagree about the same certificate,
   and a re-run disagrees with itself. The empty-SEQUENCE violation is
   simply not reported on the runs that pick FreshestCRL.

== fix
   Collect over a sorted key set rather than the map, and accumulate
   instead of returning on the first hit:

     oids := make([]string, 0, len(targetExtensionsMap))
     for oid := range targetExtensionsMap { oids = append(oids, oid) }
     sort.Strings(oids)

   Sorting alone makes the report deterministic; accumulating is what
   makes it complete. util.GetEKUStrings already sorts for exactly this
   reason -- see ZT-057, which is the same root cause reaching a
   different part of the output.

################ ZT-060-a-test-names-a-fixture-it-does-not-lint
== the file both tests name, as it sits on disk
{
  "e_dsa_correct_order_in_subgroup": {
    "result": "pass"
  }
}


Observed (zlint v3.7.1-20-g1007b1d5):

  {"e_dsa_correct_order_in_subgroup":{"result":"pass"}}

and from a checkout:

  $ go test ./lints/cabf_br/ -run 'TestDSACorrectOrderSubgroup|TestDSANotCorrectOrderSubgroup' -v
  === RUN   TestDSACorrectOrderSubgroup
  --- PASS: TestDSACorrectOrderSubgroup (0.00s)
  === RUN   TestDSANotCorrectOrderSubgroup
  --- PASS: TestDSANotCorrectOrderSubgroup (0.00s)

Both tests pass, and the file named by the one expecting Error returns pass.

test.TestLintCert is documented upstream as intentional — "useful when a unit
test reads a certificate from disk and then mutates it in some way before
trying to lint it" — so the helper is not the defect. The defect is that the
mutated case is labelled with the filename of the unmutated one, and there is
no artifact for the mutation. Three call sites do this:

lints/cabf_br/lint_dsa_correct_order_in_subgroup_test.go:44
lints/cabf_br/lint_dsa_unique_correct_representation_test.go:48
lints/community/lint_rsa_fermat_factorization_test.go:117

test.TestLintWithConfig (16 call sites) is a milder version: the same filename
carries two contradictory expectations distinguished only by a Go value.

A correct suite would serialise the mutated certificate to testdata/ and lint
it by filename, so that every stated expectation names bytes that exist. NOTE

